# Cloudflare Pages IGNORES every rule this file has ever contained.
#
# `wrangler pages dev` reports `Parsed 0 valid redirect rules` for it, and says why:
#
#   Valid status codes are 200, 301, 302 (default), 303, 307, or 308. Got 404.
#   Infinite loop detected in this rule and has been ignored.
#       at _redirects | /*  /index.html  200
#
# So the SPA fallback that this file, netlify.toml and src/routes.ts all describe as
# load-bearing was never coming from here. It is the Cloudflare Pages DEFAULT: with no
# root 404.html present, an unmatched path is served index.html with a 200. That default
# is the behaviour we want for app routes, and it is why adding a file literally named
# 404.html breaks stale shared links -- the convention takes precedence and every
# unrecognized path becomes a hard 404. The error page is therefore not-found.html.
#
# The one thing that DOES need overriding -- a real 404 for a missing archive document
# rather than the app shell with a 200 -- cannot be expressed here at all, and lives in
# functions/docs/[[path]].js and functions/data/[[path]].js. That file has the full
# reasoning.
#
# Netlify is different: it does honour a 404 status in netlify.toml, so the equivalent
# rules are written there directly. Two hosts, two mechanisms, same behaviour.
#
# Kept as documentation rather than deleted, because "there is a _redirects file so
# redirects must be configured" is the assumption that hid this for as long as it did.
